Q('m23', mk.mcq({ stem: r`An engineer compares the formula sheets of two students for the shortest distance between non-parallel lines $\vec{r} = \vec{a}_1 + \lambda\vec{b}_1$ and $\vec{r} = \vec{a}_2 + \mu\vec{b}_2$. Which formula is correct?`, opts: [r`$d = \dfrac{|(\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2) \cdot (\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1)|}{|\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2|}$`, r`$d = \dfrac{|\vec{b}_1 \cdot \vec{b}_2|}{|\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1|}$`, r`$d = |\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1|$`, r`$d = \dfrac{|(\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2) \times (\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1)|}{|\vec{b}_1 \cdot \vec{b}_2|}$`], key: 'a', sol: r`The shortest distance is the projection of $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1$ on the common perpendicular, whose direction is $\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2$. Option (c) is only the distance between the two chosen points.`, d: 1, c: 'recall', t: [5], time: 45 }), (C) => { const a1 = [0, 0, 0], b1 = [1, 2, 2], a2 = [0, 6, 0], b2 = [2, 1, -2]; C.assert(near(sdFormula(a1, b1, a2, b2), sdNumeric(a1, b1, a2, b2).d, 1e-6), 'formula agrees with minimisation'); C.key('a'); });
const m24a1 = [2, 1, 0], m24b1 = [1, -1, 1], m24a2 = [2, 1, 0], m24b2 = [3, 0, 1];
Q('m24', mk.mcq({ stem: r`Two survey sight-lines both start from the same theodolite point: $${lineVec(m24a1, m24b1)}$ and $${lineVec(m24a2, m24b2, '\\mu')}$. The shortest distance between these lines is`, opts: ['$0$', r`$\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{14}}$`, r`$\sqrt{14}$`, '$1$'], key: 'a', sol: r`Both lines pass through $(2, 1, 0)$, so they intersect there and the shortest distance is $0$ (also $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = \vec{0}$ in the formula).`, d: 2, c: 'analysis', t: [4, 5], time: 45 }), (C) => C.which([0, 1 / Math.sqrt(14), Math.sqrt(14), 1], sd(m24a1, m24b1, m24a2, m24b2)));
const m26b1 = [1, 2, 3], m26b2 = [3, 2, 1];
const m26c = cross(m26b1, m26b2); // (-4, 8, -4) ~ (1, -2, 1)
must(parallel(m26c, [1, -2, 1]), 'm26');
Q('m26', mk.mcq({ stem: r`A support beam must be perpendicular to two girders with direction ratios $1, 2, 3$ and $3, 2, 1$. Suitable direction ratios for the beam are`, opts: ['$1, -2, 1$', '$1, 2, 1$', '$4, 4, 4$', '$2, -1, 2$'], key: 'a', sol: r`$\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = (2 - 6)\hat{i} - (1 - 9)\hat{j} + (2 - 6)\hat{k} = -4\hat{i} + 8\hat{j} - 4\hat{k}$, proportional to $1, -2, 1$. Check: $1 - 4 + 3 = 0$ and $3 - 4 + 1 = 0$.`, d: 2, c: 'application', t: [2], time: 75 }), (C) => C.which([[1, -2, 1], [1, 2, 1], [4, 4, 4], [2, -1, 2]].map((d) => dot(d, m26b1) === 0 && dot(d, m26b2) === 0), true));
Q('m25', mk.mcq({ stem: r`A skylight frame edge satisfies $x = 2y = 3z$. Its direction ratios are`, opts: ['$6, 3, 2$', '$1, 2, 3$', '$2, 3, 6$', '$3, 2, 1$'], key: 'a', sol: r`Put $x = 2y = 3z = t$: the points are $\left(t, \dfrac t2, \dfrac t3\right)$, so the ratios are $1 : \dfrac12 : \dfrac13 = 6 : 3 : 2$. Option (b) uses the coefficients themselves.`, d: 3, c: 'analysis', t: [1, 3], time: 75 }), (C) => { const P = (t) => [t, t / 2, t / 3]; C.which([[6, 3, 2], [1, 2, 3], [2, 3, 6], [3, 2, 1]].map((d) => parallel(d, sub(P(6), P(0)))), true); });

// ================================================================ assertion-reason
Q('ar1', mk.ar({ a: r`A line with direction ratios $2, -1, 2$ has direction cosines $\dfrac23, -\dfrac13, \dfrac23$.`, r: r`Direction cosines are found by dividing the direction ratios by $\sqrt{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}$.`, key: 'a', sol: r`$\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = 3$, and dividing $2, -1, 2$ by $3$ gives the cosines in A; R is exactly the rule used, so it explains A.`, d: 1, c: 'understanding', t: [1] }), (C) => { C.assert(eqv(dcs([2, -1, 2]), [2 / 3, -1 / 3, 2 / 3]), 'dcs'); C.key('a'); });
Q('ar2', mk.ar({ a: r`Lines with direction ratios $1, 2, -3$ and $-2, -4, 5$ are parallel.`, r: r`Two lines are parallel when their direction ratios are proportional.`, key: 'd', sol: r`R is true. A is false: $\dfrac{-2}{1} = \dfrac{-4}{2} = -2$ but $\dfrac{5}{-3} \ne -2$, so the ratios are not proportional and the lines are not parallel.`, d: 2, c: 'analysis', t: [2] }), (C) => { C.assert(!parallel([1, 2, -3], [-2, -4, 5]) && parallel([1, 2, -3], [-2, -4, 6]), 'not proportional'); C.key('d'); });
Q('ar3', mk.ar({ a: r`The search line $\dfrac{x - 1}{2} = \dfrac{y + 3}{-1} = \dfrac{z - 4}{3}$ passes through the point $(1, -3, 4)$.`, r: r`The direction ratios of this line are $1, -3, 4$.`, key: 'c', sol: r`Putting $(1, -3, 4)$ makes every fraction $0$, so A is true. R is false: the direction ratios are the denominators $2, -1, 3$; $(1, -3, 4)$ is a point.`, d: 2, c: 'understanding', t: [3] }), (C) => { C.assert(onLine([1, -3, 4], [1, -3, 4], [2, -1, 3]) && !parallel([1, -3, 4], [2, -1, 3]), 'A true, R false'); C.key('c'); });
Q('ar4', mk.ar({ a: r`The lines $\vec{r} = \hat{i} + \lambda\hat{j}$ and $\vec{r} = 5\hat{k} + \mu\hat{i}$ are skew.`, r: r`The direction vectors $\hat{j}$ and $\hat{i}$ of these lines are not parallel.`, key: 'b', sol: r`A is true: they are not parallel and their shortest distance is $5 \ne 0$, so they do not meet. R is true, but non-parallel lines may still intersect; R alone does not explain why they are skew.`, d: 3, c: 'analysis', t: [4] }), (C) => { C.assert(!parallel([0, 1, 0], [1, 0, 0]) && near(sd([1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 5], [1, 0, 0]), 5), 'skew'); C.key('b'); });
const ar5a = [3, 0, -1];
Q('ar5', mk.ar({ a: r`The shortest distance between $\vec{r} = 3\hat{i} - \hat{k} + \lambda(2\hat{i} + \hat{j})$ and $\vec{r} = 3\hat{i} - \hat{k} + \mu(\hat{j} + 4\hat{k})$ is $0$.`, r: r`Two lines through a common point intersect.`, key: 'a', sol: r`Both lines pass through $(3, 0, -1)$, so they intersect and the shortest distance is $0$; R gives exactly this reason.`, d: 2, c: 'analysis', t: [5] }), (C) => { C.assert(near(sd(ar5a, [2, 1, 0], ar5a, [0, 1, 4]), 0), 'zero'); C.key('a'); });

// ================================================================ true-false
Q('tf1', mk.tf({ stem: r`True or false: the numbers $\dfrac{3}{13}, \dfrac{4}{13}, \dfrac{12}{13}$ can be the direction cosines of a kite string.`, key: 't', sol: r`True: $\dfrac{9 + 16 + 144}{169} = \dfrac{169}{169} = 1$, so they satisfy $l^2 + m^2 + n^2 = 1$.`, d: 1, c: 'understanding', t: [1] }), (C) => { C.assert(near(sumsq([3 / 13, 4 / 13, 12 / 13]), 1), 'sum 1'); C.key('t'); });
Q('tf2', mk.tf({ stem: r`True or false: if $a_1a_2 + b_1b_2 + c_1c_2 = 0$ for the direction ratios of two lines, the lines are parallel.`, key: 'f', sol: r`False. A zero sum of products means the lines are **perpendicular**; parallel lines have proportional direction ratios.`, d: 1, c: 'recall', t: [2] }), (C) => { C.assert(dot([2, 2, 1], [2, -1, -2]) === 0 && !parallel([2, 2, 1], [2, -1, -2]), 'perpendicular, not parallel'); C.key('f'); });
Q('tf3', mk.tf({ stem: r`True or false: two lines in space that are not parallel must meet at a point.`, key: 'f', sol: r`False. In space, non-parallel lines can be skew: for example $\vec{r} = \hat{i} + \lambda\hat{j}$ and $\vec{r} = 5\hat{k} + \mu\hat{i}$ never meet.`, d: 2, c: 'understanding', t: [4] }), (C) => { C.assert(intersect([1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 5], [1, 0, 0]) === null, 'skew example'); C.key('f'); });

// ================================================================ fill in the blank
Q('fb1', mk.fill({ stem: r`The $y$-axis has direction cosines $0$, ____, $0$.`, acc: [['1']], sol: r`The $y$-axis makes angles $90^\circ$, $0^\circ$, $90^\circ$ with the axes, so its direction cosines are $0, 1, 0$.`, d: 1, c: 'recall', t: [1] }), (C) => { C.blank(1); C.assert(eqv(dcs([0, 4, 0]), [0, 1, 0]), 'dcs of y-axis'); });
Q('fb2', mk.fill({ stem: r`In the equation $\vec{r} = \vec{a} + \lambda\vec{b}$ of a line, the vector $\vec{b}$ is called the ____ vector of the line.`, acc: [['direction', 'parallel']], sol: r`$\vec{b}$ is the direction (parallel) vector: every point of the line is reached from $\vec{a}$ by moving a multiple of $\vec{b}$.`, d: 1, c: 'recall', t: [3] }), (C) => C.blank('direction'));
Q('fb3', mk.fill({ stem: r`A road on a flyover and a road on the ground beneath it are straight, not parallel, and never cross. As lines in space they are called ____ lines.`, acc: [['skew']], sol: r`Such lines are called skew lines; they do not lie in one plane.`, d: 1, c: 'recall', t: [4] }), (C) => C.blank('skew'));

// ================================================================ numerical
const n01P = [1, 2, -3], n01Q = [7, 4, 0];
Q('n01', mk.num({ stem: r`A guy wire is stretched straight from $P(1, 2, -3)$ to $Q(7, 4, 0)$ (metres). Find its length in metres.`, value: norm(sub(n01Q, n01P)), unit: 'm', sol: r`$PQ = \sqrt{6^2 + 2^2 + 3^2} = \sqrt{49} = 7$ m.`, scheme: [['7 m', 1]], d: 1, c: 'understanding', t: [1], time: 60 }), (C) => C.val(Math.hypot(6, 2, 3)));
const n02a = [3, -5, 1], n02b = [2, 3, 3];
Q('n02', mk.num({ stem: r`The point $(k, 1, 7)$ lies on the straight cable $${cart(n02a, n02b)}$. Find $k$.`, value: 7, sol: r`From $y$: $\dfrac{1 + 5}{3} = 2$; from $z$: $\dfrac{7 - 1}{3} = 2$. So $\lambda = 2$ and $x = 3 + 2 \times 2 = 7$, i.e. $k = 7$.`, scheme: [['k = 7', 1]], d: 2, c: 'understanding', t: [3], time: 90 }), (C) => { C.val(n02a[0] + 2 * n02b[0]); C.assert(onLine([7, 1, 7], n02a, n02b), 'on line'); });
const n03a = [2, -1, 0], n03b = [2, 1, -2], n03P = [8, 5, 0];
const n03F = foot(n03P, n03a, n03b);
must(n03F.t === 2 && eqv(n03F.F, [6, 1, -4]), 'n03 foot');
Q('n03', mk.num({ stem: r`A sensor at $P(8, 5, 0)$ monitors a straight conveyor $${lineVec(n03a, n03b)}$. Find the perpendicular distance of the sensor from the conveyor.`, value: norm(sub(n03P, n03F.F)), sol: r`Foot: $\lambda = \dfrac{(6, 6, 0) \cdot (2, 1, -2)}{9} = 2$, so $F = (6, 1, -4)$. Distance $PF = \sqrt{2^2 + 4^2 + 4^2} = 6$.`, scheme: [['6', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [4], time: 150 }), (C) => { C.val(norm(cross(sub(n03P, n03a), n03b)) / norm(n03b)); C.assert(near(dot(sub(n03P, n03F.F), n03b), 0), 'perpendicular'); });
const n04a1 = [1, -1, 2], n04b1 = [1, 2, 2], n04a2 = [1, 5, 2], n04b2 = [2, 1, -2];
const n04 = sd(n04a1, n04b1, n04a2, n04b2);
must(near(n04, 4), `n04 ${n04}`);
Q('n04', mk.num({ stem: r`Two drones fly along $${lineVec(n04a1, n04b1)}$ and $${lineVec(n04a2, n04b2, '\\mu')}$ (units in metres). Find the shortest distance between their paths.`, value: 4, unit: 'm', sol: r`$\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = -6\hat{i} + 6\hat{j} - 3\hat{k}$, $|\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2| = 9$; $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = 6\hat{j}$ gives a dot product of $36$; $d = \dfrac{36}{9} = 4$ m.`, scheme: [['4 m', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [5], time: 180 }), (C) => { C.val(sdNumeric(n04a1, n04b1, n04a2, n04b2).d, 1e-6); C.assert(eqv(cross(n04b1, n04b2), [-6, 6, -3]), 'cross'); });
Q('n05', mk.num({ stem: r`For which value of $k$ is a line with direction ratios $k, 2, 3$ perpendicular to a line with direction ratios $2, -5, 4$?`, value: -1, sol: r`$2k - 10 + 12 = 0 \Rightarrow 2k = -2 \Rightarrow k = -1$.`, scheme: [['k = −1', 1]], d: 2, c: 'understanding', t: [2], time: 60 }), (C) => C.val([-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2].find((k) => dot([k, 2, 3], [2, -5, 4]) === 0)));

// ================================================================ short answer, 2 marks
const s201A = [-2, 4, 1], s201B = [4, 1, 3];
Q('s201', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`A zipline runs straight from $A(-2, 4, 1)$ to $B(4, 1, 3)$. Find the direction cosines of the zipline $AB$.`, model: r`Direction ratios: $4 - (-2) = 6$, $1 - 4 = -3$, $3 - 1 = 2$. Length $\sqrt{36 + 9 + 4} = 7$.\n\nDirection cosines: $${dcTex(sub(s201B, s201A))}$.`, sol: 'Differences of coordinates, then divide by the length.', scheme: [['Ratios 6, −3, 2 and length 7', 1], ['Direction cosines', 1]], d: 1, c: 'understanding', t: [1], time: 90 }), (C) => { C.assert(eqv(dcs(sub(s201B, s201A)), [6 / 7, -3 / 7, 2 / 7]), 'dcs'); C.model([6, 3, 2, 7]); });
const s202A = [1, -2, 3], s202B = [3, 1, 2], s202C = [7, 7, 0];
Q('s202', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`Three poles of a straight fence are planned at $A(1, -2, 3)$, $B(3, 1, 2)$ and $C(7, 7, 0)$. Use direction ratios to show that the poles are in one straight line.`, model: r`$AB$: $2, 3, -1$. $BC$: $4, 6, -2 = 2(2, 3, -1)$.\n\nThe ratios are proportional, so $AB \parallel BC$; since they share the point $B$, the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are collinear.`, sol: 'Proportional ratios plus a common point.', scheme: [['Ratios of AB and BC', 1], ['Proportional with common point ⇒ collinear', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [1], time: 90 }), (C) => { C.assert(parallel(sub(s202B, s202A), sub(s202C, s202B)) && onLine(s202C, s202A, sub(s202B, s202A)), 'collinear'); C.model([2, 3, 1, 4, 6]); });
Q('s203', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`A hang-glider's two tow lines follow $\dfrac{x}{2} = \dfrac{y}{2} = \dfrac{z}{1}$ and $\dfrac{x - 5}{4} = \dfrac{y + 2}{1} = \dfrac{z - 3}{8}$. Find the angle between the tow lines.`, model: r`Ratios $2, 2, 1$ (length $3$) and $4, 1, 8$ (length $9$).\n\n$\cos\theta = \dfrac{|8 + 2 + 8|}{3 \times 9} = \dfrac{18}{27} = \dfrac23$, so $\theta = \cos^{-1}\dfrac23$.`, sol: 'Use the cosine formula with the direction ratios.', scheme: [['Dot product 18 and lengths 3, 9', 1], ['θ = cos⁻¹(2/3)', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [2], time: 120 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(Math.cos((angleDeg([2, 2, 1], [4, 1, 8]) * Math.PI) / 180), 2 / 3), 'cos 2/3'); C.model([18, 27, r`\dfrac23`]); });
const s204P = [2, -3, 5], s204b = [4, -1, 3];
Q('s204', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`A new pipe must pass through the valve at $P(2, -3, 5)$ and run parallel to the existing pipe $\dfrac{x - 1}{4} = \dfrac{y + 2}{-1} = \dfrac{z}{3}$. Write the vector and Cartesian equations of the new pipe.`, model: r`Direction ratios $4, -1, 3$ (from the existing pipe).\n\nVector: $${lineVec(s204P, s204b)}$.\n\nCartesian: $${cart(s204P, s204b)}$.`, sol: 'Same direction as the given line, through the new point.', scheme: [['Vector equation', 1], ['Cartesian equation', 1]], d: 1, c: 'understanding', t: [3], time: 90 }), (C) => { C.assert(onLine(s204P, s204P, s204b) && parallel(s204b, [4, -1, 3]), 'line'); C.model(['x - 2', 'y + 3', 'z - 5']); });
const s205A = [-1, 3, 2], s205B = [2, 5, -4], s205d = sub(s205B, s205A);
const s205X = at(s205A, s205d, 1 / 3);
must(near(s205X[2], 0) && near(s205X[0], 0) && near(s205X[1], 11 / 3), `s205 ${s205X}`);
Q('s205', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`A mine shaft is drilled in a straight line from $A(-1, 3, 2)$ towards $B(2, 5, -4)$. Find the point where the shaft meets the $xy$-plane.`, model: r`Line: $${cart(s205A, s205d)} = \lambda$, so a general point is $(-1 + 3\lambda, 3 + 2\lambda, 2 - 6\lambda)$.\n\nOn the $xy$-plane $z = 0$: $\lambda = \dfrac13$, giving $\left(0, \dfrac{11}{3}, 0\right)$.`, sol: 'General point of the line, then set z = 0.', scheme: [['General point', 1], ['(0, 11/3, 0)', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [1, 3], time: 120 }), (C) => { C.assert(onLine(s205X, s205A, s205d), 'on line'); C.model([r`\dfrac{11}{3}`, r`\dfrac13`]); });
const s206a1 = [2, 0, 1], s206b1 = [1, 1, -1], s206a2 = [3, 2, 1], s206b2 = [1, 2, 0];
const s206t = dot(sub(s206a2, s206a1), cross(s206b1, s206b2));
must(s206t === 0 && eqv(cross(s206b1, s206b2), [2, -1, 1]), 's206');
Q('s206', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`Show that the two cable runs $${lineVec(s206a1, s206b1)}$ and $${lineVec(s206a2, s206b2, '\\mu')}$ lie in one plane.`, model: r`$\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = 2\hat{i} - \hat{j} + \hat{k}$, $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = \hat{i} + 2\hat{j}$.\n\n$(\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1) \cdot (\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2) = 2 - 2 + 0 = 0$, so the lines are coplanar.`, sol: 'Scalar triple product equal to zero.', scheme: [['b₁ × b₂', 1], ['Triple product 0 ⇒ coplanar', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [4], time: 120 }), (C) => { C.assert(intersect(s206a1, s206b1, s206a2, s206b2) !== null, 'they meet, so coplanar'); C.model([2, 1, 0]); });
const s207a1 = [1, -1, 0], s207a2 = [4, 2, -3], s207b = [2, -1, 2];
const s207 = sd(s207a1, s207b, s207a2, s207b);
must(near(s207, Math.sqrt(26)) && eqv(cross(s207b, sub(s207a2, s207a1)), [-3, 12, 9]), `s207 ${s207}`);
Q('s207', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`Two parallel rails of an imagined funicular are $${cart(s207a1, s207b)}$ and $${cart(s207a2, s207b)}$. Find the distance between the rails.`, model: r`$\vec{b} = 2\hat{i} - \hat{j} + 2\hat{k}$, $|\vec{b}| = 3$; $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = 3\hat{i} + 3\hat{j} - 3\hat{k}$.\n\n$\vec{b} \times (\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1) = -3\hat{i} + 12\hat{j} + 9\hat{k}$, of length $\sqrt{234} = 3\sqrt{26}$.\n\n$d = \dfrac{3\sqrt{26}}{3} = \sqrt{26}$.`, sol: 'Parallel-lines formula.', scheme: [['Cross product and its length', 1], ['d = √26', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [5], time: 150 }), (C) => C.model([234, 26]));
const s208P = [1, -1, 2], s208Q = [3, 4, -2], s208R = [0, 3, 2], s208S = [3, 5, 6];
Q('s208', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`A beam joins $P(1, -1, 2)$ to $Q(3, 4, -2)$, and a strut joins $R(0, 3, 2)$ to $S(3, 5, 6)$. Show that the beam is perpendicular to the strut.`, model: r`$PQ$: $2, 5, -4$. $RS$: $3, 2, 4$.\n\n$2(3) + 5(2) + (-4)(4) = 6 + 10 - 16 = 0$, so $PQ \perp RS$.`, sol: 'Zero sum of products of direction ratios.', scheme: [['Both sets of ratios', 1], ['Sum of products 0', 1]], d: 1, c: 'understanding', t: [2], time: 90 }), (C) => { C.assert(dot(sub(s208Q, s208P), sub(s208S, s208R)) === 0, 'perpendicular'); C.model([6, 10, 16]); });
const s209a = [1, -3, 2], s209b = [1, 2, 0];
const s209F = foot([0, 0, 0], s209a, s209b);
must(s209F.t === 1 && eqv(s209F.F, [2, -1, 2]), 's209');
Q('s209', mk.short({ marks: 2, stem: r`A surveyor at the origin looks at a straight boundary wall $${lineVec(s209a, s209b)}$. Find the foot of the perpendicular from the origin to the wall and its distance from the origin.`, model: r`General point $F = (1 + \lambda, -3 + 2\lambda, 2)$. $\vec{OF} \cdot (1, 2, 0) = 0$: $(1 + \lambda) + 2(-3 + 2\lambda) = 0 \Rightarrow 5\lambda = 5 \Rightarrow \lambda = 1$.\n\n$F = (2, -1, 2)$, distance $\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = 3$.`, sol: 'Perpendicularity condition gives λ.', scheme: [['λ = 1 and F', 1], ['Distance 3', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [4], time: 120 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(norm(s209F.F), 3) && near(dot(s209F.F, s209b), 0), 'foot'); C.model([2, 1, 3]); });

// ================================================================ short answer, 3 marks
const c60 = Math.cos(Math.PI / 3);
const s301g = 1 - c60 * c60 - c60 * c60;
must(near(s301g, 0.5), 's301');
Q('s301', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`A camera drone's line of sight makes $60^\circ$ with the $x$-axis and $60^\circ$ with the $y$-axis. Find the angle it makes with the $z$-axis (both possibilities) and its direction cosines in each case.`, model: r`$\cos^2 60^\circ + \cos^2 60^\circ + \cos^2\gamma = 1 \Rightarrow \dfrac14 + \dfrac14 + \cos^2\gamma = 1 \Rightarrow \cos^2\gamma = \dfrac12$.\n\n$\cos\gamma = \pm\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$, so $\gamma = 45^\circ$ or $135^\circ$.\n\nDirection cosines: $\dfrac12, \dfrac12, \dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$ or $\dfrac12, \dfrac12, -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$.`, sol: 'Use l² + m² + n² = 1.', scheme: [['cos²γ = 1/2', 1], ['γ = 45° or 135°', 1], ['Both sets of direction cosines', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [1], time: 150 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(sumsq([0.5, 0.5, Math.SQRT1_2]), 1) && near(Math.acos(Math.SQRT1_2) * 180 / Math.PI, 45), 'gamma'); C.model([45, 135]); });
const s302p = 4 / 5;
must(near(dot([2, s302p, -1], [s302p, 3, 4]), 0), 's302');
Q('s302', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`Two straight hiking trails are $\dfrac{x + 1}{2} = \dfrac{y - 3}{p} = \dfrac{z}{-1}$ and $\dfrac{x - 4}{p} = \dfrac{y + 2}{3} = \dfrac{z - 1}{4}$. Find $p$ if the trails are perpendicular, and then write integer direction ratios of the first trail.`, model: r`Perpendicular: $2p + 3p + (-1)(4) = 0 \Rightarrow 5p = 4 \Rightarrow p = \dfrac45$.\n\nFirst trail ratios: $2, \dfrac45, -1$; multiply by $5$: $10, 4, -5$.`, sol: 'Sum of products equals zero; then clear the fraction.', scheme: [['Condition 2p + 3p − 4 = 0', 1], ['p = 4/5', 1], ['Ratios 10, 4, −5', 1]], d: 2, c: 'application', t: [2], time: 150 }), (C) => { C.assert(parallel([2, s302p, -1], [10, 4, -5]), 'ratios'); C.model([r`\dfrac45`, 10, 4, 5]); });
// (2x - 3)/4 = (1 - y)/2 = (3z + 6)/9  ->  (x - 3/2)/2 = (y - 1)/(-2) = (z + 2)/3
Q('s303', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`A light rail line is recorded as $\dfrac{2x - 3}{4} = \dfrac{1 - y}{2} = \dfrac{3z + 6}{9}$. Write it in standard Cartesian form, state a point on it and its direction ratios, and write its vector equation.`, model: r`Divide to make each coefficient $1$: $\dfrac{x - \frac32}{2} = \dfrac{y - 1}{-2} = \dfrac{z + 2}{3}$.\n\nPoint $\left(\dfrac32, 1, -2\right)$; direction ratios $2, -2, 3$.\n\nVector form: $\vec{r} = \dfrac32\hat{i} + \hat{j} - 2\hat{k} + \lambda(2\hat{i} - 2\hat{j} + 3\hat{k})$.`, sol: 'Standard form first, then read point and ratios.', scheme: [['Standard form', 1], ['Point and ratios', 1], ['Vector equation', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [3], time: 180 }), (C) => {
  const P = (t) => [(4 * t + 3) / 2, 1 - 2 * t, (9 * t - 6) / 3];
  C.assert(eqv(P(0), [1.5, 1, -2]) && parallel(sub(P(1), P(0)), [2, -2, 3]), 'point and ratios');
  C.model([r`\dfrac32`, 2, 3]);
});
const s304a1 = [2, -1, 3], s304b1 = [1, 2, -1], s304a2 = [-1, 3, -4], s304b2 = [2, -1, 3];
const s304X = intersect(s304a1, s304b1, s304a2, s304b2);
must(s304X && eqv(s304X.P, [3, 1, 2]) && s304X.s === 1 && s304X.t === 2, `s304 ${JSON.stringify(s304X)}`);
Q('s304', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`Two irrigation pipes follow $${cart(s304a1, s304b1)}$ and $${cart(s304a2, s304b2)}$. Show that they meet and find the junction point.`, model: r`General points: $(2 + \lambda, -1 + 2\lambda, 3 - \lambda)$ and $(-1 + 2\mu, 3 - \mu, -4 + 3\mu)$.\n\nEquate $x$ and $y$: $2 + \lambda = -1 + 2\mu$ and $-1 + 2\lambda = 3 - \mu$, giving $\lambda = 1$, $\mu = 2$.\n\nCheck $z$: $3 - 1 = 2$ and $-4 + 6 = 2$ ✓. The pipes meet at $(3, 1, 2)$.`, sol: 'Solve two coordinate equations; verify the third.', scheme: [['General points', 1], ['λ = 1, μ = 2', 1], ['Third equation checked; point (3, 1, 2)', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [4], time: 210 }), (C) => { C.assert(onLine([3, 1, 2], s304a1, s304b1) && onLine([3, 1, 2], s304a2, s304b2), 'point on both'); C.model([3, 1, 2]); });
const s305a = [-1, 2, 3], s305b = [2, -2, 1], s305P = [2, 2, 6];
const s305F = foot(s305P, s305a, s305b);
must(s305F.t === 1 && eqv(s305F.F, [1, 0, 4]) && near(norm(sub(s305P, s305F.F)), 3), 's305');
Q('s305', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`A Wi-Fi router at $P(2, 2, 6)$ is to be wired to the nearest point of a straight cable tray $${cart(s305a, s305b)}$. Find that nearest point (the foot of the perpendicular) and the length of wire needed.`, model: r`General point $F = (-1 + 2\lambda, 2 - 2\lambda, 3 + \lambda)$; $\vec{PF} = (2\lambda - 3, -2\lambda, \lambda - 3)$.\n\n$\vec{PF} \cdot (2, -2, 1) = 0$: $4\lambda - 6 + 4\lambda + \lambda - 3 = 0 \Rightarrow 9\lambda = 9 \Rightarrow \lambda = 1$.\n\n$F = (1, 0, 4)$; length $PF = \sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = 3$.`, sol: 'Perpendicular condition, then the distance.', scheme: [['PF in terms of λ', 1], ['λ = 1, F = (1, 0, 4)', 1], ['Length 3', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [4], time: 210 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(norm(cross(sub(s305P, s305a), s305b)) / norm(s305b), 3), 'distance by cross product'); C.model([1, 0, 4, 3]); });
const s306a1 = [1, 2, 1], s306b1 = [1, -1, 1], s306a2 = [2, -1, -1], s306b2 = [2, 1, 2];
const s306 = sd(s306a1, s306b1, s306a2, s306b2);
const s306c = cross(s306b1, s306b2);
must(eqv(s306c, [-3, 0, 3]) && dot(sub(s306a2, s306a1), s306c) === -9 && near(s306, 9 / Math.sqrt(18)), `s306 ${s306c} ${s306}`);
Q('s306', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`Two straight ducts in an imagined factory ceiling are $${lineVec(s306a1, s306b1)}$ and $${lineVec(s306a2, s306b2, '\\mu')}$. Show that the ducts are skew.`, model: r`Directions $1, -1, 1$ and $2, 1, 2$ are not proportional, so the ducts are not parallel.\n\n$\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = -3\hat{i} + 3\hat{k}$, $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = \hat{i} - 3\hat{j} - 2\hat{k}$; $(\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1) \cdot (\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2) = -3 - 6 = -9 \ne 0$.\n\nSo the shortest distance $\dfrac{9}{3\sqrt2} = \dfrac{3}{\sqrt2} \ne 0$: the ducts never meet and are skew.`, sol: 'Not parallel, and the triple product is non-zero.', scheme: [['Not parallel', 1], ['Triple product −9', 1], ['Non-zero distance ⇒ skew', 1]], d: 3, c: 'analysis', t: [4, 5], time: 210 }), (C) => { C.assert(!parallel(s306b1, s306b2) && intersect(s306a1, s306b1, s306a2, s306b2) === null, 'skew'); C.model([9, r`\dfrac{3}{\sqrt2}`]); });
const s307a1 = [1, 0, 2], s307b1 = [1, 2, 2], s307a2 = [3, 1, 0], s307b2 = [2, -2, 1];
const s307 = sd(s307a1, s307b1, s307a2, s307b2);
must(near(s307, 3) && eqv(cross(s307b1, s307b2), [6, 3, -6]), `s307 ${s307}`);
Q('s307', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`Two zipline cables at an adventure park follow $${lineVec(s307a1, s307b1)}$ and $${lineVec(s307a2, s307b2, '\\mu')}$ (metres). Find the shortest distance between the cables.`, model: r`$\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = 6\hat{i} + 3\hat{j} - 6\hat{k}$, $|\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2| = 9$.\n\n$\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = 2\hat{i} + \hat{j} - 2\hat{k}$; dot product $12 + 3 + 12 = 27$.\n\n$d = \dfrac{27}{9} = 3$ m.`, sol: 'Skew-lines formula.', scheme: [['b₁ × b₂ and its length 9', 1], ['Dot product 27', 1], ['d = 3 m', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [5], time: 210 }), (C) => C.model([9, 27, 3]));
const s308X = [2, 1, 3], s308b1 = [1, 3, -1], s308a1 = sub(s308X, s308b1), s308b2 = [2, -1, 1], s308a2 = add(s308X, s308b2);
must(eqv(s308a1, [1, -2, 4]) && eqv(s308a2, [4, 0, 4]) && near(sd(s308a1, s308b1, s308a2, s308b2), 0), 's308');
Q('s308', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`A lab technician aims two laser pointers along $${cart(s308a1, s308b1)}$ and $${cart(s308a2, s308b2)}$. Compute the shortest distance between the beams and say what it tells you.`, model: r`$\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = (3 - 1)\hat{i} - (1 + 2)\hat{j} + (-1 - 6)\hat{k} = 2\hat{i} - 3\hat{j} - 7\hat{k}$.\n\n$\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = 3\hat{i} + 2\hat{j}$; dot product $6 - 6 + 0 = 0$.\n\nSo $d = 0$: the beams meet (at $(2, 1, 3)$).`, sol: 'Zero shortest distance with non-parallel directions means intersecting lines.', scheme: [['Cross product', 1], ['Dot product 0', 1], ['Conclusion: beams intersect', 1]], d: 3, c: 'analysis', t: [4, 5], time: 210 }), (C) => { C.assert(eqv(cross(s308b1, s308b2), [2, -3, -7]) && onLine(s308X, s308a1, s308b1) && onLine(s308X, s308a2, s308b2), 'meet'); C.model([2, 3, 7, 0]); });
const s309P = [2, -1, 3], s309b1 = [2, 1, -1], s309b2 = [1, -1, 2], s309c = cross(s309b1, s309b2);
must(eqv(s309c, [1, -5, -3]), 's309');
Q('s309', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`A drainage channel must start at $(2, -1, 3)$ and be perpendicular to two existing channels with direction ratios $2, 1, -1$ and $1, -1, 2$. Find its vector equation.`, model: r`Direction $\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = (2 - 1)\hat{i} - (4 + 1)\hat{j} + (-2 - 1)\hat{k} = \hat{i} - 5\hat{j} - 3\hat{k}$.\n\nCheck: $2 - 5 + 3 = 0$ and $1 + 5 - 6 = 0$.\n\n$${lineVec(s309P, s309c)}$.`, sol: 'Cross product gives a direction perpendicular to both.', scheme: [['Cross product', 1], ['Perpendicularity checked', 1], ['Vector equation', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [2, 3], time: 180 }), (C) => { C.assert(dot(s309c, s309b1) === 0 && dot(s309c, s309b2) === 0, 'perpendicular'); C.model([5, 3]); });
Q('s310', mk.short({ marks: 3, stem: r`In a classroom model of a room, write (i) the Cartesian equations of the $y$-axis, (ii) the vector equation of the $x$-axis, and (iii) the equation of the $yz$-plane.`, model: r`(i) $y$-axis: $x = 0$, $z = 0$ (equivalently $\dfrac x0 = \dfrac y1 = \dfrac z0$).\n\n(ii) $x$-axis: $\vec{r} = \lambda\hat{i}$.\n\n(iii) $yz$-plane: $x = 0$.`, sol: 'Points of each axis or plane have the stated coordinates zero.', scheme: [['y-axis', 1], ['x-axis', 1], ['yz-plane', 1]], d: 1, c: 'recall', t: [1], time: 120 }), (C) => { C.assert([[0, 2, 0], [0, -5, 0]].every((p) => p[0] === 0 && p[2] === 0), 'y-axis points'); C.model([r`\lambda\hat{i}`, 'x = 0']); });

// ================================================================ long answer, 5 marks (in parts)
const l01P = [2, 1, -3], l01Q = [5, -1, 3], l01R = [1, 0, 0], l01S = [3, 6, 1];
const l01u = sub(l01Q, l01P);
must(eqv(l01u, [3, -2, 6]) && norm(l01u) === 7 && dot(l01u, sub(l01S, l01R)) === 0 && dot(l01u, [2, 3, 6]) === 36, 'l01');
Q('l01', mk.long({ stem: r`A straight power line runs from tower $P(2, 1, -3)$ to tower $Q(5, -1, 3)$.\n\n(a) Find the direction cosines of $PQ$. [2]\n\n(b) Show that $PQ$ is perpendicular to the cable joining $R(1, 0, 0)$ and $S(3, 6, 1)$. [1]\n\n(c) Find the angle between $PQ$ and a support wire with direction ratios $2, 3, 6$. [2]`, model: r`**(a)** Ratios $3, -2, 6$; $PQ = \sqrt{9 + 4 + 36} = 7$. Direction cosines $${dcTex(l01u)}$.\n\n**(b)** $RS$: $2, 6, 1$. $3(2) + (-2)(6) + 6(1) = 6 - 12 + 6 = 0$, so $PQ \perp RS$.\n\n**(c)** $|2, 3, 6| = 7$: $\cos\theta = \dfrac{|6 - 6 + 36|}{7 \times 7} = \dfrac{36}{49}$, so $\theta = \cos^{-1}\dfrac{36}{49}$.`, sol: 'Ratios and length; zero dot product; cosine formula.', scheme: [['Ratios and length 7', 1], ['Direction cosines', 1], ['Perpendicularity', 1], ['Dot product 36 and lengths', 1], ['θ = cos⁻¹(36/49)', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [1, 2], time: 480 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(Math.cos(angleDeg(l01u, [2, 3, 6]) * Math.PI / 180), 36 / 49), 'angle'); C.model([7, r`\dfrac{36}{49}`, 12]); });
const l02X = [1, 3, -2], l02b1 = [2, -1, 2], l02b2 = [2, 2, -1], l02a1 = sub(l02X, l02b1), l02a2 = add(l02X, mul(2, l02b2));
const l02I = intersect(l02a1, l02b1, l02a2, l02b2);
must(eqv(l02a1, [-1, 4, -4]) && eqv(l02a2, [5, 7, -4]) && l02I && eqv(l02I.P, l02X) && dot(l02b1, l02b2) === 0, `l02 ${JSON.stringify(l02I)}`);
Q('l02', mk.long({ stem: r`Two straight tunnels of an imagined hill railway are $${cart(l02a1, l02b1)}$ and $${cart(l02a2, l02b2)}$.\n\n(a) Show that the tunnels meet and find the point where they meet. [3]\n\n(b) Find the angle between the tunnels. [2]`, model: r`**(a)** General points: $(-1 + 2\lambda, 4 - \lambda, -4 + 2\lambda)$ and $(5 + 2\mu, 7 + 2\mu, -4 - \mu)$.\n\nFrom $x$: $2\lambda - 2\mu = 6$; from $y$: $-\lambda - 2\mu = 3$. Subtracting, $3\lambda = 3$, so $\lambda = 1$, $\mu = -2$.\n\n$z$: $-4 + 2 = -2$ and $-4 + 2 = -2$ ✓. They meet at $(1, 3, -2)$.\n\n**(b)** $2(2) + (-1)(2) + 2(-1) = 0$, so the angle is $90^\circ$.`, sol: 'Solve two equations, check the third; dot product of directions.', scheme: [['General points', 1], ['λ = 1, μ = −2', 1], ['Check and point (1, 3, −2)', 1], ['Dot product 0', 1], ['Angle 90°', 1]], d: 4, c: 'analysis', t: [4, 2], time: 540 }), (C) => { C.assert(onLine(l02X, l02a1, l02b1) && onLine(l02X, l02a2, l02b2) && near(angleDeg(l02b1, l02b2), 90), 'meet at right angles'); C.model([1, 3, 2, 90]); });
const l03a = [-1, 1, 0], l03b = [2, 1, 2], l03P = [5, 7, 0];
const l03F = foot(l03P, l03a, l03b), l03img = sub(mul(2, l03F.F), l03P);
must(l03F.t === 2 && eqv(l03F.F, [3, 3, 4]) && near(norm(sub(l03P, l03F.F)), 6) && eqv(l03img, [1, -1, 8]), `l03 ${JSON.stringify(l03F)} ${l03img}`);
Q('l03', mk.long({ stem: r`A security camera at $P(5, 7, 0)$ watches a straight fence $${lineVec(l03a, l03b)}$.\n\n(a) Find the foot of the perpendicular from $P$ to the fence and the perpendicular distance. [3]\n\n(b) A mirror image of the camera is to be drawn on the other side of the fence, at the reflection of $P$ in the line. Find its coordinates. [2]`, model: r`**(a)** General point $F = (-1 + 2\lambda, 1 + \lambda, 2\lambda)$; $\vec{PF} = (2\lambda - 6, \lambda - 6, 2\lambda)$.\n\n$\vec{PF} \cdot (2, 1, 2) = 4\lambda - 12 + \lambda - 6 + 4\lambda = 9\lambda - 18 = 0 \Rightarrow \lambda = 2$.\n\n$F = (3, 3, 4)$; $PF = \sqrt{4 + 16 + 16} = 6$.\n\n**(b)** $F$ is the midpoint of $P$ and its image $P'$: $P' = 2F - P = (6 - 5, 6 - 7, 8 - 0) = (1, -1, 8)$.`, sol: 'Foot by perpendicularity; image by the midpoint formula.', scheme: [['PF in terms of λ', 1], ['λ = 2 and F', 1], ['Distance 6', 1], ['Midpoint idea', 1], ['Image (1, −1, 8)', 1]], d: 4, c: 'application', t: [4], time: 540 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(norm(cross(sub(l03P, l03a), l03b)) / norm(l03b), 6) && onLine(l03F.F, l03a, l03b), 'distance check'); C.model([3, 4, 6, 1, 8]); });
const l04a1 = [2, -1, 1], l04b1 = [2, 2, 1], l04a2 = [3, -1, -1], l04b2 = [1, -2, 2];
const l04 = sd(l04a1, l04b1, l04a2, l04b2);
must(near(l04, 2) && eqv(cross(l04b1, l04b2), [6, -3, -6]), `l04 ${l04}`);
Q('l04', mk.long({ stem: r`Two cable cars cross a valley along $${lineVec(l04a1, l04b1)}$ and $${lineVec(l04a2, l04b2, '\\mu')}$ (units: tens of metres).\n\n(a) Find the shortest distance between the two cable lines. [3]\n\n(b) Write both lines in Cartesian form and state, with a reason, whether the cables can touch. [2]`, model: r`**(a)** $\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = (4 + 2)\hat{i} - (4 - 1)\hat{j} + (-4 - 2)\hat{k} = 6\hat{i} - 3\hat{j} - 6\hat{k}$, of length $9$.\n\n$\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = \hat{i} - 2\hat{k}$; dot product $6 + 0 + 12 = 18$.\n\n$d = \dfrac{18}{9} = 2$ units, that is $20$ m.\n\n**(b)** $${cart(l04a1, l04b1)}$ and $${cart(l04a2, l04b2)}$.\n\nThe shortest distance is not zero (and the directions are not parallel), so the lines are skew: the cables cannot touch.`, sol: 'Skew-lines formula; non-zero distance means no meeting point.', scheme: [['Cross product and length 9', 1], ['Dot product 18', 1], ['d = 2 units (20 m)', 1], ['Cartesian forms', 1], ['Skew, so no contact', 1]], d: 4, c: 'application', t: [5, 3], time: 540 }), (C) => { C.assert(intersect(l04a1, l04b1, l04a2, l04b2) === null, 'skew'); C.model([9, 18, 2, 20]); });
const l05a1 = [0, 0, 10], l05b1 = [1, -2, 2], l05a2 = [0, 3, 16], l05b2 = [2, 2, 1];
const l05 = sd(l05a1, l05b1, l05a2, l05b2);
must(near(l05, 5) && eqv(cross(l05b1, l05b2), [-6, 3, 6]) && intersect(l05a1, l05b1, l05a2, l05b2) === null, `l05 ${l05}`);
Q('l05', mk.long({ stem: r`Two delivery drones over an imagined town move in straight lines: drone A along $${lineVec(l05a1, l05b1)}$ and drone B along $${lineVec(l05a2, l05b2, '\\mu')}$ (metres).\n\n(a) By equating coordinates, show that the paths do not cross. [2]\n\n(b) Find the closest distance between the two paths. [3]`, model: r`**(a)** $x$: $\lambda = 2\mu$; $y$: $-2\lambda = 3 + 2\mu$. Then $-4\mu = 3 + 2\mu$, so $\mu = -\dfrac12$, $\lambda = -1$.\n\n$z$: $10 + 2\lambda = 8$ but $16 + \mu = 15.5$; these differ, so the paths do not meet.\n\n**(b)** $\vec{b}_1 \times \vec{b}_2 = -6\hat{i} + 3\hat{j} + 6\hat{k}$, of length $9$; $\vec{a}_2 - \vec{a}_1 = 3\hat{j} + 6\hat{k}$, dot product $9 + 36 = 45$.\n\n$d = \dfrac{45}{9} = 5$ m.`, sol: 'Inconsistent third equation; then the skew-lines formula.', scheme: [['λ and μ from two equations', 1], ['Third equation fails', 1], ['Cross product and length 9', 1], ['Dot product 45', 1], ['d = 5 m', 1]], d: 4, c: 'analysis', t: [4, 5], time: 540 }), (C) => { C.assert(near(10 + 2 * -1, 8) && near(16 - 0.5, 15.5), 'z values differ'); C.model([45, 9, 5, 8]); });
const l06P = [1, -1, 2], l06b1 = [1, 1, 1], l06b2 = [1, -1, 0], l06c = cross(l06b1, l06b2);
must(eqv(l06c, [1, 1, -2]) && onLine([4, 2, -4], l06P, l06c), 'l06');
Q('l06', mk.long({ stem: r`A stage designer needs a light bar through $P(1, -1, 2)$ that is perpendicular to two existing bars with directions $\hat{i} + \hat{j} + \hat{k}$ and $\hat{i} - \hat{j}$.\n\n(a) Find the vector and Cartesian equations of the light bar. [3]\n\n(b) Will the light bar pass through the hook at $(4, 2, -4)$? Justify. [2]`, model: r`**(a)** Direction $(\hat{i} + \hat{j} + \hat{k}) \times (\hat{i} - \hat{j}) = (0 + 1)\hat{i} - (0 - 1)\hat{j} + (-1 - 1)\hat{k} = \hat{i} + \hat{j} - 2\hat{k}$.\n\nVector: $${lineVec(l06P, l06c)}$. Cartesian: $${cart(l06P, l06c)}$.\n\n**(b)** At $(4, 2, -4)$: $\dfrac{3}{1} = \dfrac{3}{1} = \dfrac{-6}{-2} = 3$. All three fractions are equal, so the bar passes through the hook ($\lambda = 3$).`, sol: 'Cross product for the direction; substitute the point.', scheme: [['Cross product', 1], ['Vector equation', 1], ['Cartesian equation', 1], ['Substitution', 1], ['Conclusion λ = 3', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [3, 2], time: 480 }), (C) => { C.assert(dot(l06c, l06b1) === 0 && dot(l06c, l06b2) === 0, 'perpendicular to both'); C.model([3, 2]); });
